  m_text: The inferior angle is the lowest point of the scapula, formed where the
    medial (vertebral) border meets the lateral (axillary) border. Serving as a significant
    anatomical landmark, it points downward and aids in determining the movement
    and rotation of the scapula. Importantly, the inferior angle provides an attachment
    site for the teres major muscle, which plays a role in medially rotating and
    adducting the arm.
