      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The mandible, commonly known as the lower jawbone, is the largest and strongest
    bone in the face. It forms the lower part of the skull and holds the lower teeth
    in place. Articulating at the temporal bones via the temporomandibular joints,
    it facilitates mouth opening, speaking, and chewing. The mandible consists of
    a horizontal body and two vertical rami. Important landmarks include the mental
    foramen, alveolar process, and the mandibular condyle. Injuries or disorders
    of the mandible can impact speech, chewing, and facial aesthetics, often necessitating
    medical or surgical intervention.
