  m_text: 'The thoracic vertebrae form the middle segment of the vertebral column,
    situated between the cervical and lumbar regions. Humans typically have twelve
    thoracic vertebrae, labeled T1 through T12. Distinctive characteristics of the
    thoracic vertebrae include:

    Costal Facets: Present on both the body and
    transverse processes of most thoracic vertebrae, these facets articulate with
    the heads and tubercles of the ribs, respectively.

    Spinous Processes: Generally
    long and pointed downward, they limit excessive back extension.

    Vertebral
    Body: It''s relatively larger than that of the cervical but smaller than the
    lumbar vertebrae.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
