      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The subscapular fossa is a broad, concave surface found on the anterior
    (ventral) side of the scapula. This large fossa occupies most of the scapula's
    costal surface. It serves as the origin site for the subscapularis muscle, a
    powerful internal rotator of the humerus and one of the four crucial muscles
    of the rotator cuff. The muscle fibers of the subscapularis arise from the fossa's
    ridges and intertubercular sulcus of the humerus. Given its role, the integrity
    and condition of the subscapular fossa and the muscle originating from it are
    critical in assessing and understanding shoulder function and possible injuries.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
