      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The sternum, commonly referred to as the breastbone, is a flat, elongated
    bone located in the center of the anterior thoracic cage. Comprising three primary
    segments, it includes the manubrium (top), the body (middle), and the xiphoid
    process (small, pointed lower end). The sternum articulates laterally with the
    costal cartilages of the first seven ribs and with the clavicles superiorly,
    forming the sternal-clavicular joint. Serving as a protective shield for the
    heart, lungs, and major blood vessels, the sternum also provides attachment sites
    for specific chest and neck muscles. Its position and integrity are crucial when
    assessing thoracic deformities or performing medical procedures like cardiopulmonary
    resuscitation (CPR).
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
