  m_text: 'The occipital bone is located at the back of the skull, forming part of
    the cranial cavity''s base. It consists of two parts: the basilar and lateral
    parts, and features a large opening called the foramen magnum, through which
    the spinal cord connects to the brain. The occipital condyles enable articulation
    with the first cervical vertebra. Fractures or malformations of this bone can
    lead to significant neurological issues.'
