      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The infraspinous fossa is a broad, slightly concave surface located on
    the posterior aspect of the scapula, below its distinguishing spine. It's bordered
    by the spine, the medial border, and the lateral border of the scapula. This
    fossa is primarily occupied by the infraspinatus muscle, which is one of the
    four essential rotator cuff muscles that play a role in stabilizing and rotating
    the shoulder joint. The infraspinatus muscle originates from this fossa, stretching
    laterally to attach to the greater tubercle of the humerus. The condition and
    size of the infraspinous fossa can be vital in musculoskeletal evaluations related
    to shoulder injuries or conditions.
