  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The sternal angle, often referred to as the "angle of Louis", is a prominent,
    palpable horizontal ridge located where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum.
    It marks the level of the second rib's costal cartilage attachment to the sternum
    and corresponds to the location where the second rib meets the spine. Clinically
    significant, the sternal angle serves as a key anatomical landmark for rib counting
    and indicates the level of the intervertebral disc between the fourth and fifth
    thoracic vertebrae. It also denotes the bifurcation of the trachea and the beginning
