      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The left radius is one of the two long bones of the forearm, positioned
    on the lateral side when the palm faces forward. It extends from the elbow to
    the wrist and runs parallel to the ulna, its counterpart in the forearm. At its
    proximal end, the radius has a rounded head that rotates around the ulna, facilitating
    the forearm's ability to pronate and supinate. Distally, it broadens and forms
    the primary joint with the carpal bones of the wrist. Its structural design and
    articulations play a pivotal role in wrist movement and stability. Due to its
    location and functions, the radius is susceptible to fractures, especially from
    falls onto an outstretched hand, commonly referred to as Colles' fractures.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
