  m_text: The atlas is the first cervical vertebra, commonly referred to as C1. Unlike
    other vertebrae, the atlas lacks a body and spinous process. Instead, it's characterized
    by a ring-like structure with an anterior and posterior arch. Its primary function
    is to support the skull, allowing nodding and the "yes" motion of the head. Two
    large concave facets on the atlas articulate with the occipital condyles of the
    skull. Another distinguishing feature is the lateral masses on either side, which
    house the superior and inferior articular facets. The atlas rotates around the
    dens of the axis (C2) to enable the head's side-to-side movement.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
